  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nA stationary frontal boundary currently bisects central \nIllinois just south of the I-72 corridor. Moisture transport \nalong this boundary remains robust, with observed precipitable \nwater values around 1.8 to 2.0 inches, exceeding the the \npercentile for mid-September. Regional radar demonstrates \nelevated convection rooted above the nocturnal boundary layer \nwell north of the front, north of the I-74 corridor.\n\nHeavy rainfall across the far northern CWA is the primary \nforecast concern through this morning as the 850 mb jet \nincreases to around 35 kt. A small, but notable shift northward \nin QPF swaths has been seen in the 00z runs of the HREF and \nREFS. This places the highest probability of heavy rain just \nnorth of the local area near the I-80 corridor through mid \nmorning. In these areas swaths of 2-4\" are shown in PMM \nproducts. Will leave the Flood Watch in place through 15z for \nKnox, Stark, and Marshall Counties in case the band of heavier \nrain ends up shifting farther south, but overall that risk is \nnow lower. \n\nThe front is forecast to remain stationary through this \nafternoon. South of the front, highs will reach the mid 90s, \nwith some upper 90s south of I-70. These temps combined with \ndewpoints in the lower 70s will yield heat indices peaking \naround 105 degrees. Have expanded the Heat Advisory a row of \ncounties north to include the Springfield and Jacksonville \nareas. Isolated pulse convective development is possible this \nafternoon and evening near the front, or outflow from morning \nstorms. Without much of a trigger have kept PoPs low at 20-30%.\n\nA sharp north to south temperature gradient remains over the\narea Friday with the front laid out west-to east near the middle\nof the state. This will result in highs ranging from the lower\n80s north of I-74 to the mid 90s south of I-70. Dewpoints are\nforecast to be a few degrees lower which should keep peak heat\nindices at or below 100 degrees south of the front. Will keep\nlow chance PoPs in the forecast with the front in the area,\nthough with a lack of upper support coverage should be low.\n\nGuidance shows good agreement that the front temporarily lifts\njust north of the area on Saturday, as the flow buckles\nsouthwest ahead of a mid level trough digging through the\ncentral Rockies. As a result PoPs have been lowered and highs\nraised into the 90s CWA-wide.\n\nShower and thunderstorm coverage is forecast to increase for \nthe Saturday night through Monday period, when forcing for \nascent increases ahead of the slow-moving upper level wave. \nLatest NBM shows likely (60%) PoPs for this timeframe north of \nI-70. \n\nMedium range ensemble guidance shows good agreement of a major\npattern change for early next week. Upper flow transitions to\nnorthwesterly as the trough digs across the Great Lakes. This is\nforecast to bring a surge of cooler and drier air into the \nregion, with the NBM advertising highs in the 70s north of I-70 \nMon-Wed.",
